= Common Errors and Special Cases

#exception-box(title: "Common Errors & Special Cases")[
  #table(
    columns: (1.35fr, 1.35fr, 1.2fr),
    table.header[*The mistake students make*][*What is right*][*The why / the trap*],
    ["The objective is profit maximisation"], [It is *wealth maximisation* — long-term value, adjusted for risk and time], [A firm can raise this year's profit by starving maintenance — wealth would fall while profit rises.],
    ["Dividends are paid because profit exists"], [Dividends need *cash* — a profitable firm with tight cash flow may skip the payout], [Profit is an accounting result; the dividend decision checks the cash drawer.],
    ["Trading on equity always helps"], [It amplifies returns *only when return exceeds the cost of debt* — and earnings are stable], [When earnings fall, the same debt amplifies losses instead.],
    ["Capital structure and financial planning are the same"], [Planning decides *how much and from where* overall; capital structure is the *debt–equity mix* of long-term funds], [One is the blueprint, the other is one section of it.],
    ["Fixed and working capital factors are interchangeable"], [Fixed = nature, scale, technique, growth, technology, cycles; working = credit policy, seasons, efficiency, inflation], [Swapped factor lists are the unit's most common multiple-choice trap.],
    ["More debt is always cheaper, so always borrow"], [Debt costs less but raises *fixed obligations and financial risk*], [The mix balances cost against risk and control — not cost alone.],
  )
]

- *Spot the pattern:* factor-list questions are *sorting* questions — read the
  situation, ask which decision or which capital the factor moves, then
  answer from that list alone.
- *One special case:* when a question says "the firm's EBIT covers its
  interest bill eight times" — the tested factor is the *interest coverage
  ratio*, pushing the firm toward *more debt* in its capital structure.

